2. Network Discovery and Reconnaissance

Network reconnaissance identifies hosts, exposed services, and the structure of an authorized network.

"Nmap" — Network Mapper

Nmap is a central tool for host discovery, port scanning, service detection, and basic OS fingerprinting.

Discover live hosts on a lab subnet:

nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24

Scan selected TCP ports:

nmap -p 22,53,80,443,445 192.168.1.10

Detect service versions:

nmap -sV 192.168.1.10

Run the default safe-oriented script category against a lab target:

nmap -sC -sV 192.168.1.10

Save results in several formats:

nmap -sV -oA lab-scan 192.168.1.10

The command creates output files with ".nmap", ".gnmap", and ".xml" extensions.

Important: Service detection and scripts can generate traffic or interact with services. Use them only within the approved scope.

"arp-scan" — Local Network Discovery

sudo arp-scan --localnet

Specify an interface if needed:

sudo arp-scan --interface=eth0 --localnet

ARP discovery is useful on local IPv4 Ethernet networks. It does not discover hosts across routers in the same way as a subnet scan.

"netdiscover" — LAN Discovery

sudo netdiscover

For a known lab subnet:
Use case: Quickly investigate devices visible on a local network. Interface names and available options depend on the environment.

"traceroute" and "mtr" — Route Analysis

traceroute example.com

For a continuously updated route and latency view:

mtr example.com

Use case: Investigate routing paths, latency, and possible packet loss. Routers may block or deprioritize probe traffic, so missing responses do not automatically indicate a broken connection.

