3. Nmap: Advanced Port Scanning

Nmap provides several scanning methods. Choose the least intrusive scan that answers the question.

Scan a specific port range

nmap -p 1-1000 192.168.1.10

Scan the most common ports

nmap --top-ports 100 192.168.1.10

Scan TCP ports with SYN scanning

sudo nmap -sS -p 1-1000 192.168.1.10

SYN scanning generally requires elevated privileges for raw packet access.

Scan UDP services

sudo nmap -sU -p 53,67,123,161 192.168.1.10

UDP scanning can be slow and ambiguous because some services do not respond to probes.

Examine a known service

nmap -sV -p 22 192.168.1.10

Use selected NSE scripts

List scripts installed with Nmap:

ls /usr/share/nmap/scripts/

Search for scripts related to a service:

ls /usr/share/nmap/scripts/ | grep -i http

Run a specific script only after reviewing its documentation and confirming that it is appropriate for the target.

Interpret common states

State| Meaning
"open"| A service is accepting connections on the port.
"closed"| The host responded, but no service is listening there.
"filtered"| Nmap cannot determine the state because filtering or missing responses interfere.
open| filtered

Security lesson: An open port is not proof of a vulnerability. Identify the service, verify its configuration, and investigate relevant evidence.

