7. Web and API Security: Burp Suite

Burp Suite is a web security testing platform. It is especially useful for inspecting HTTP requests and responses, testing authentication, and analyzing APIs.

Core components

Component| Purpose
Proxy| Intercepts and inspects HTTP/S traffic from a configured client.
Repeater| Re-sends manually modified requests for controlled testing.
Intruder| Automates customized request variations within an authorized scope.
Decoder| Encodes and decodes common data formats.
Comparer| Compares requests, responses, and other data.
Logger / HTTP history| Helps review recorded traffic, depending on edition and version.

Recommended lab workflow

1. Start Burp Suite.
2. Use a dedicated browser profile configured for Burp's proxy.
3. Open a deliberately vulnerable application or authorized test API.
4. Review the request and response in Proxy history.
5. Send an interesting request to Repeater.
6. Change one parameter at a time.
7. Compare status codes, headers, response bodies, and timing.
8. Record the finding and explain its security impact.

Inspect an API with "curl"

Make a basic GET request:

curl -i https://example.com/api/

Send JSON to an authorized test endpoint:

curl -i -X POST https://example.com/api/test \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"name":"test"}'

The example endpoint is illustrative; substitute an endpoint you are authorized to test.

"mitmproxy" — Intercepting Proxy

mitmproxy --help

"mitmproxy" provides command-line and web-based options for examining HTTP traffic. Client configuration and certificate trust must be handled carefully, particularly when HTTPS is involved.

Security lesson: Focus on broken access control, authentication weaknesses, input validation, insecure data exposure, and incorrect API behavior. Never test real users' accounts or data without explicit authorization.

