9. Network Connections and Troubleshooting

"ping" — Basic Reachability

ping -c 4 192.168.1.1

ICMP may be filtered, so a failed ping does not necessarily mean the host is offline.

"nc" — Netcat

Check whether a TCP port accepts a connection:

nc -vz 192.168.1.10 22

Connect to a local test service:

nc 127.0.0.1 8000

Netcat is useful for diagnosing connectivity and testing simple client/server behavior. Avoid exposing test listeners to untrusted networks.

"socat" — Flexible Socket Relay

socat -h

Socat supports many address types and protocols. Because it can relay traffic between endpoints, carefully review any relay configuration before using it.

"curl" — HTTP and Network Diagnostics

Inspect response headers:

curl -I https://example.com

Show detailed connection information:

curl -v https://example.com

Set a timeout:

curl --connect-timeout 5 --max-time 15 https://example.com

"ssh" — Secure Remote Administration

Connect to a host you administer:

ssh user@192.168.1.10

Inspect server host-key fingerprints through the appropriate trusted administrative process when verifying a new connection.

Security lesson: Diagnose DNS, routing, TCP reachability, TLS, and application responses as separate layers rather than assuming every failure is a network failure.

